 <section class="changed">
 <h2>What changed</h2>
-<p>There is no new rule and no compliance deadline. No owner is obliged to sell, no lender is obliged to finance, and no customer has budget because succession exists. The clock is demographic and voluntary. In an ABN AMRO/Ipsos I&amp;O survey of 519 entrepreneurs, 68% expected to stop steering within ten years. Among that group, 22% had not started succession and 20% were searching without a successor. <!-- CHECK: 05-succession-demand --></p>
+<p>There is no new rule and no compliance deadline. No owner is obliged to sell, no lender is obliged to finance, and no customer has budget because succession exists. The clock is demographic and voluntary. In a December 2025 ABN AMRO/Ipsos I&amp;O survey of 519 entrepreneurs, 68% expected to stop steering within ten years. Among that group, 22% had not started succession and 20% were searching without a successor. <!-- CHECK: 05-succession-demand --></p>
 <p>Wholesale is relevant, but not automatically attractive. ABN's CBS-based table counted 5,210 owners aged 65 or older in wholesale and trade intermediation, or 15.7% of owners of firms with at least two working people. <!-- CHECK: 05-wholesale-age --> Brookz displayed 95 wholesale businesses for sale when checked. That is seller behaviour on one marketplace, not completed deals or evidence that any listing fits this buy box. <!-- CHECK: 05-marketplace --></p>
 <p>Demand for the acquisition therefore means a seller opening records, accepting a transition and negotiating risk allocation. The ABN survey supports a future supply of conversations. The marketplace supports present availability. Neither proves transferable cash flow. The budget appears only when a lender underwrites one target and the seller leaves money contingent or subordinated.</p>
 <p>Sector averages provide a warning rather than a valuation. CBS reported 2024 wholesale revenue of €648.435 billion and pre-tax result of €34.481 billion, a 5.3% aggregate margin. The series covers all sizes, so large firms dominate it; it is not a small-target margin. <!-- CHECK: 05-wholesale-margin --> A buyer who needs a heroic margin expansion to service debt has not found an owner-independent company. They have financed a turnaround.</p>
@@ -44,8 +44,8 @@
 <h2>Why you</h2>
 <p>The useful edges are narrower than “developer buys boring business.” English, Polish and Russian help qualify CEE suppliers and speak directly with parts makers. Regulatory literacy helps reject products with hidden importer duties. An engineering habit helps expose stock, quote, picking and quality failures as measurable work. The audience can produce introductions to industrial buyers and operators. None of those edges proves that this reader can retain a Dutch customer book.</p>
 <p>The Dutch-language gate is commercial. A2 is not enough for an angry local buyer, a performance conversation with an employee or a subtle price negotiation. <!-- CHECK: 05-language-gate --> The gate passes only if a Dutch account owner remains under a signed employment and retention arrangement, leads the top-customer handover and already owns routine relationships. It fails if “the team speaks Dutch” but every exception still goes to the seller. The buyer should attend those calls, improve Dutch and remain accountable. Translation is not customer ownership.</p>
-<p>Hammond Chemicals is the useful precedent elsewhere. Euston Ventures acquired the UK blender, packager and distributor of industrial solvents. Search-fund entrepreneur Jim Ward became chief executive, while the third Hammond generation stayed in leadership. <!-- CHECK: 05-precedent-deal --> The independent Companies House trace lists Hammond Chemicals Limited as active, incorporated in 1969 and engaged in wholesale of chemical products. <!-- CHECK: 05-precedent-trace --> It proves the operating shape: an outside CEO, lender-backed succession and family leaders retained.</p>
-<p>It does not prove affordability. The transaction price and cash flow were not public in the sources used here, and the acquirer had sector knowledge and institutional support. <!-- CHECK: 05-precedent-deal --> This reader should copy the retention logic, not infer a multiple. The personal constraint is equally sharp: search can run beside the job, but operation cannot. On closing day the software job ends. If employment must continue, do not sign.</p>
+<p>Hammond Chemicals is a useful precedent elsewhere. According to lender Shawbrook's case study, Euston Ventures acquired the UK blender, packager and distributor of industrial solvents. Jim Ward became chief executive, third-generation family members remained in leadership, and Shawbrook supported the transaction. <!-- CHECK: 05-precedent-deal --> The independent Companies House trace lists Hammond Chemicals Limited as active, incorporated in 1969 and engaged in wholesale of chemical products. <!-- CHECK: 05-precedent-trace --> Together, the sources illustrate a CEO change with family leaders retained. They do not independently verify the transaction structure. <!-- CHECK: 05-precedent-limit --></p>
+<p>The sources used here publish no transaction price or target cash flow. <!-- CHECK: 05-precedent-limit --> They therefore support no comparable multiple. Copy only the retained-leadership pattern. The personal constraint is equally sharp: search can run beside the job, but operation cannot. On closing day the software job ends. If employment must continue, do not sign.</p>
 </section>
 
