 <h2>Does the money work?</h2>
 <p>The number to settle first was acquisition finance for a first-time buyer with €100,000. The public part is now clear. Qredits publishes an MKB loan of €50,000–€250,000, 9.95% annual interest, a 1.5% fee, monthly linear or annuity payments, an average five-year term and a maximum ten-year term. It expressly includes company acquisition as a use. <!-- CHECK: 05-qredits-terms --></p>
-<p>The missing part remains decisive. Qredits says it assesses the completed application, plan and financial information after a personal conversation. It does not publish an approval formula for this first-time buyer or promise ten years for an acquisition. <!-- CHECK: 05-qredits-terms --> BMKB does not settle that gap. RVO says a starter is under three years, a starter loan can be at most €333,333, and the state guarantee is 67.5%. The scheme enlarges collateral; it is not a loan offer. <!-- CHECK: 05-bmkb --></p>
+<p>The missing part remains decisive. Qredits says it assesses the completed application, plan and financial information after a personal conversation. The reviewed public record does not settle an approval formula for this buyer or an acquisition-specific ten-year promise. <!-- CHECK: 05-public-record-limit --> BMKB does not settle that gap. RVO says a starter is under three years, a starter loan can be at most €333,333, and the state guarantee is 67.5%. The scheme enlarges collateral; it is not a loan offer. <!-- CHECK: 05-bmkb --></p>
 <p>Qredits' currently linked MKB conditions add the critical term. All business assets serve as collateral. When the borrower is a legal person, Qredits asks its directors to sign a guarantee for joint liability. <!-- CHECK: 05-qredits-security --> A full €160,000 personal guarantee would put €210,000 at risk after the €50,000 cash commitment. That fails the confirmed risk cap. The Qredits route survives only if the signed offer limits total personal recourse to the remaining headroom.</p>
 <p>The actual bank rate, guarantee fee and permitted seller-note terms remain unavailable without a target and lender decision. The amount of personal recourse in a specific Qredits offer is also unavailable. <!-- CHECK: 05-public-record-limit --> Therefore the model uses Qredits' published rate and five-year amortisation only as a debt-service stress. It assumes no lender approval. A written indicative response to this model is a ninety-day pass condition.</p>
-<p>Every amount below except the Qredits product terms is a design hurdle, not a market price or forecast. The purchase-price hurdle is €300,000: €40,000 buyer cash, a €160,000 senior loan and a €100,000 seller note subordinated until the senior loan is repaid. Lender fees, due diligence and transaction work have a separate €10,000 hurdle. Cash committed is €50,000; no further personal recourse is allowed. KVK describes stacked acquisition finance and gives an example with one-quarter seller-subordinated funding repaid after bank debt. <!-- CHECK: 05-seller-finance --></p>
+<p>Every amount below except the Qredits product terms is a design hurdle, not a market price or forecast. The purchase-price hurdle is €300,000: €40,000 buyer cash, a €160,000 senior loan and a €100,000 seller note. The note is subordinated during the senior loan, carries 5% cash interest and then repays in five annual €20,000 instalments. Those invented terms require written lender and seller acceptance.</p>
+<p>Lender fees, diligence and transaction work have a separate €10,000 hurdle. The ninety-day budget comes from that allowance, rather than sitting above it. Cash committed remains €50,000, and no further personal recourse is allowed. KVK describes stacked acquisition finance and illustrates seller-subordinated funding repaid after bank debt. <!-- CHECK: 05-seller-finance --></p>
